Confirmed admission-test evidence gap
The diagnostic emits no delegate-admission-error event at all, while both delegateRejectsOversizedAnnouncedOrUnknownBody arguments pass. The two corresponding remaining direct-read requests received watchdog errors before their delayed response callbacks selected allow=false. Thus the earlier source-level ambiguity is exercised in this diagnostic: its passing admission declaration does not prove either intended header/body admission path. Tighten that test to identify its intended denial and establish the necessary delivery phase; do not simply treat resourceLimitExceeded as sufficient when deadline failure uses the same category. Preserve the separate deadline regressions. Full10's uninstrumented admission path remains unobserved.

Observer limits and next step
The tracer buffers callback events per instance and does not print while inside delegate/protocol/watchdog callbacks. Nevertheless, read/test exits synchronously flush all stores and print under a global output mutex; allocations, locks and output can perturb scheduling and occupy an executing task. Diagnostic stores are retained until process exit. The four additional existing-test issues make it especially inappropriate to treat this run as a clean baseline or exact performance result. No callback trace is an independent TLS or JPIP peer oracle.

The smallest informative next step is a separate diagnostic-only canary on the actual assigned serial OperationQueue, scheduled independently of the delegate callback that may be waiting, plus process/thread sampling during the observed interval if available. Timestamp canary enqueue and execution: promptly executed canaries with late delegate callbacks point upstream; late canaries locate queue execution delay. Preserve the current limits/concurrency and account for instrumentation overhead. Do not assume a production QoS or thread change is justified by clustered timestamps alone. A later repair must be tested on the ordinary uninstrumented candidate and retain all required assertions and deadlines; neither harness scheduling assumptions nor correct elapsed-deadline rejection automatically waive the existing failed gate.

